"Oh, that a man's reach should exceed his grasp, or
what's a heaven for?" -- Robert Browning.

You have to work hard to see this message, but there
are several ways to do it.  I got it by turning on
autopickup to grab a wand off a hole in a Sokoban
level above the bottom level (so I didn't lose the
wand by burying it with a boulder), jumping through
the hole, and happening to land on a left-over
boulder on the level below:

  "You cannot get your hand around this boulder."

It took me longer to figure out _why_ I had gotten
that message (I was trying to autopickup a boulder,
a bit beyond my grip capacity) and what it meant,
than it took me to get into that situation in the
first place.

The rest of the story for why I was doing this the
hard way was that I couldn't step far enough away
from the hole simply to knock the wand through with
a thrown object; stuff I threw landed past the hole.

This is one of those "the dev team thinks of
everything" situations, though in this case they
were perhaps thinking of being teleported onto a
boulder, rather than jumping through a hole to land
on it.  I suppose dismounting onto a boulder with
autopickup engaged would have the same, confusing,
effect, but for some reason it is limited, in all
cases, to Sokoban.

The pertinent code is in pickup.c, but the message
is a constructed one; search on "around" to find it
quickly.

